 "standard_text": "Find the squares of numbers 1 to 20; identify perfect squares; find the square roots of perfect squares up to 400. (summary of the NERDC Primary 4 Mathematics curriculum)",
 "meta_desc": "Free Primary 4 maths worksheets on squares of numbers 1 to 20, perfect squares and square roots up to 400, with area and arrangement problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Square = number × itself",
 "key_sub": "Square root: which number times itself gives this?",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>The square of a number is the number multiplied by itself: 7² = 7 × 7 = 49.</li><li>Numbers like 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … are perfect squares.</li><li>The square root (√) undoes squaring: √49 = 7 because 7 × 7 = 49.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>15²</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>15 × 15</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 225</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>√324</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>18 × 18 = 324</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 18</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Doubling instead of squaring</td><td>6² = 36, not 12.</td></tr><tr><td>Halving to find a square root</td><td>√64 = 8, not 32.</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking (a + b)² = a² + b²</td><td>Work out each square separately.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "Why are they called square numbers?",
   "a": "They make square arrangements: 16 dots make a 4 by 4 square."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is √1?",
   "a": "1, because 1 × 1 = 1."
  },
  {
   "q": "Which squares should I know?",
   "a": "From 1² = 1 to 20² = 400."
  }
